{"name":"Galaxy Green","hex":"#79afad","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"galaxy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The Milky Way; the apparent band of concentrated stars which appears in the night sky over earth. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any of the collections of many millions or billions of stars, galactic dust, black holes, etc. existing as independent and coherent systems, of which there are billions in the known universe."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To furnish with galaxies.","To gather together into a luminous whole."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/galaxy"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Galaxy","blocks":[{"text":"A galaxy is a physical system of stars, planetary systems, stellar remnants, substellar objects, interstellar gas, dust, and dark matter bound together by gravity. The word is derived from the Greek galaxias (γαλαξίας), meaning 'milky', a reference to the Milky Way galaxy that contains the Solar System. Galaxies, averaging an estimated 100 million stars, range in size from dwarfs with less than a thousand stars to the largest galaxies known—supergiants with one hundred trillion stars, each orbiting its galaxy's center of mass. Most of the mass in a typical galaxy is in the form of dark matter, with only a few percent of that mass visible in the form of stars and nebulae. Supermassive black holes are a common feature at the centers of galaxies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/NGC_4414_%28NASA-med%29.jpg/330px-NGC_4414_%28NASA-med%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galaxy","wikidata":"Q318"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Galaxy cluster SDSS J1152+3313. SDSS stands for Sloan Digital Sky Survey, J for Julian epoch, and 1152+3313 for right ascension and declination respectively.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AProbing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Herschel-Galaxy.png/500px-Herschel-Galaxy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Herschel-Galaxy.png/500px-Herschel-Galaxy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The shape of the Milky Way as estimated from star counts by William Herschel in 1785; the Solar System was assumed to be near the center.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHerschel-Galaxy.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Pic_iroberts1.jpg/500px-Pic_iroberts1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Pic_iroberts1.jpg/500px-Pic_iroberts1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Photograph of the \"Great Andromeda Nebula\" by Isaac Roberts, 1899, later identified as the Andromeda Galaxy","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APic_iroberts1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Andromeda_galaxy.jpg/500px-Andromeda_galaxy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Andromeda_galaxy.jpg/1280px-Andromeda_galaxy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Andromeda_galaxy.jpg/500px-Andromeda_galaxy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut